<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="NutManager.App.Presentation.Controls.NutStatusLed"
             x:Name="Root"
             Width="32"
             Height="32"
             ClipToBounds="False">
  <!--
    One lit ball, not a stack of circles. Earlier versions layered a second and third ellipse
    around the core to suggest a glow, but an ellipse has a hard edge: concentric ellipses read as
    rings drawn around the dot rather than as light coming off it. The glow is a blurred drop
    shadow instead, so it fades out with no contour of its own.

    A shadow colour cannot come from a brush binding, so each state carries its own class. The halo
    is a bare 6px border with no fill - only its shadow is ever visible - and pulsing the ball means
    animating that shadow's opacity and scale from the render thread through the Composition API,
    which costs nothing on the UI thread.

    The halo is deliberately smaller than the 10px core and its shadow carries no spread, so the
    shadow always originates inside the core and the glow emerges from under it. A halo as wide as
    the core, or any spread at all, starts the shadow outside the core's edge and leaves a dark gap
    ringing the ball - which is the ring look this layout exists to avoid.

    That constraint also caps the pulse: the halo is 6px and the core 10px, so the shadow's hole
    grows with it and at 1.6 the two are within a fraction of a pixel of each other, which brings a
    dark rim back at the peak of every breath. 1.45 keeps roughly half a pixel of overlap, and the
    swell is carried mostly by opacity anyway.

    Sizes are even numbers inside an even cell so every layer centres on a whole pixel. A
    fractional size leaves the circle straddling a half pixel and the antialiasing comes out
    heavier on one side, which at this diameter reads as the ball sitting crooked.

    The cell is 32px for a 10px ball because the glow has to fit inside it. At full swell the
    shadow reaches about 15px from the centre, and a cell any tighter than that crops the round
    glow against its own straight edges - which is where the square outline around the light came
    from. The extra width is empty space, not a bigger indicator.

    Colour and animation are decorative; the state is always spelled out in the adjacent text and
    in the accessible name.
  -->
  <UserControl.Styles>
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-halo">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="999" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-halo.healthy">
      <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="0 0 11 0 #F24ADE80" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-halo.pending">
      <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="0 0 11 0 #F2FBBF24" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-halo.critical">
      <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="0 0 11 0 #F2F87171" />
    </Style>
  </UserControl.Styles>

  <Grid Width="32" Height="32" Background="Transparent" ClipToBounds="False">
    <Border x:Name="Halo"
            Classes="nut-led-halo"
            Width="6"
            Height="6"
            Opacity="0"
            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
            VerticalAlignment="Center"
            IsHitTestVisible="False" />
    <Ellipse x:Name="Core"
             Width="10"
             Height="10"
             Fill="{Binding StateBrush, ElementName=Root}"
             HorizontalAlignment="Center"
             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
    <!-- Lens highlight: upper-left quadrant, deliberately faint so it never reads as gloss. -->
    <Ellipse x:Name="Highlight"
             Width="2"
             Height="2"
             Fill="White"
             Opacity="0.34"
             Margin="0,0,4,4"
             HorizontalAlignment="Center"
             VerticalAlignment="Center"
             IsHitTestVisible="False" />
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
